Below is a minimal, self‑contained snippet that shows how you can invoke the **`validateReactProp`** helper to check your `serviceProvider` object before you pass it to the `UserProfile` component.

```js
// 1️⃣  Import the helper that performs the safety checks
import { validateReactProp } from 'functions';

// 2️⃣  Assume you already have the object you want to validate
//     (e.g. fetched from an API, created locally, etc.)
const serviceProvider = /* your object here */;

// 3️⃣  Convert the object to a JSON string – the helper expects a
//     string representation of the value.
const serviceProviderString = JSON.stringify(serviceProvider);

// 4️⃣  Call the validator.  It will:
//
//      • Warn if `serviceProvider` is a Promise instance.
//      • Warn if the object contains any own properties that are
//        functions (methods) – these could trigger side‑effects
//        when rendered as a prop.
//
//      The validator returns `undefined`; any warnings are logged
//      to the console (or handled internally by the helper).
validateReactProp({
  obj: serviceProviderString,
  componentName: 'UserProfile',
});

// 5️⃣  If no warning was emitted, you can safely pass the object
//     to the component.
<UserProfile serviceProvider={serviceProvider} />
```

### What the helper does under the hood

| Check | What it looks for | Why it matters |
|-------|-------------------|----------------|
| **Promise instance** | `obj instanceof Promise` | Promises are asynchronous and can’t be rendered directly; they would break React’s rendering flow. |
| **Method properties** | Any own property whose value is a function (`typeof value === 'function'`) | Methods can trigger side‑effects (e.g., network calls, state updates) when the component re‑renders, violating React’s pure‑prop contract. |

If either of these conditions is true, the helper will emit a warning (typically to `console.warn`) so you can fix the issue before the component mounts.

Feel free to adjust the snippet to fit your build system or testing framework. Happy coding!

